1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,634
Four United States Navy sailors confessed to the same rape and murder — one after

2
00:00:04,634 --> 00:00:07,895
another, facing the same detective, in Norfolk, Virginia.

3
00:00:08,195 --> 00:00:09,495
Signed statements.

4
00:00:09,795 --> 00:00:11,049
Recorded details.

5
00:00:11,349 --> 00:00:14,182
Four separate men, all guilty by their own words.

6
00:00:14,482 --> 00:00:15,643
Here is the problem.

7
00:00:15,943 --> 00:00:17,243
None of them did it.

8
00:00:17,543 --> 00:00:22,286
The DNA said so every single time, and every time it said so, the detective across

9
00:00:22,286 --> 00:00:24,137
the table did not release a man.

10
00:00:24,437 --> 00:00:26,341
He went out and found a new one.

11
00:00:26,641 --> 00:00:28,824
This story has five confessions in it.

12
00:00:29,124 --> 00:00:30,517
Four of them were false.

13
00:00:30,817 --> 00:00:35,204
Keep count — because the man who collected the false ones ends this story in a

14
00:00:35,204 --> 00:00:36,947
federal prison cell of his own.

15
00:00:38,747 --> 00:00:41,023
Her name was Michelle Moore-Bosko.

16
00:00:41,323 --> 00:00:46,301
She was eighteen years old, newly married to a Navy sailor, living in an apartment

17
00:00:46,301 --> 00:00:49,822
near the Norfolk naval base while her husband went to sea.

18
00:00:50,122 --> 00:00:55,471
On the night of July 7th, 1997, someone she knew came to her door — and by morning

19
00:00:55,471 --> 00:01:00,755
she was gone, and one of the worst false-confession disasters in American history

20
00:01:00,755 --> 00:01:01,407
had begun.

21
00:01:01,707 --> 00:01:05,190
Before this is over, eight men in all will be charged with killing her.

22
00:01:05,490 --> 00:01:07,348
Seven of them will be innocent.

23
00:01:07,648 --> 00:01:10,806
The one guilty man will not be caught by detectives at all.

24
00:01:11,106 --> 00:01:16,257
He will mail his confession from a prison cell, in his own handwriting, almost as a

25
00:01:16,257 --> 00:01:21,284
joke — and the astonishing thing, the thing this film exists to show you, is what

26
00:01:21,284 --> 00:01:24,574
the system did when the truth arrived in an envelope.

27
00:01:24,874 --> 00:01:25,989
It did nothing.

28
00:01:26,289 --> 00:01:28,332
So keep counting confessions with me.

29
00:01:28,632 --> 00:01:33,276
And keep one eye on the detective, because his ending is the one nobody saw coming.

30
00:01:35,076 --> 00:01:39,813
Norfolk, Virginia is a Navy town the way some towns are steel towns or coal towns.

31
00:01:40,113 --> 00:01:42,110
The harbor is gray with warships.

32
00:01:42,410 --> 00:01:47,259
Half the young men you meet are sailors, and half the doors in the apartment blocks

33
00:01:47,259 --> 00:01:52,108
near the base belong to Navy families — young couples, new marriages, husbands gone

34
00:01:52,108 --> 00:01:53,277
for weeks at a time.

35
00:01:53,577 --> 00:01:58,226
It is a place built on absences: you marry at nineteen, you learn to sleep alone

36
00:01:58,226 --> 00:02:02,991
half the year, and the people across the hall become your whole world because they

37
00:02:02,991 --> 00:02:05,373
are the only world that doesn't ship out.

38
00:02:05,673 --> 00:02:08,320
Michelle and Billy Bosko were one of those couples.

39
00:02:08,620 --> 00:02:11,406
He was a teenager himself, out on a week at sea.

40
00:02:11,706 --> 00:02:14,400
She was eighteen, waiting for him to come home.

41
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:15,768
Seven days.

42
00:02:16,068 --> 00:02:17,926
That was the length of the deployment.

43
00:02:18,226 --> 00:02:22,545
That is how fast a life can be standing there one week and gone the next.

44
00:02:22,845 --> 00:02:28,469
On July 8th, 1997, Billy Bosko came home from the sea, opened his own front door,

45
00:02:28,469 --> 00:02:30,136
and found his wife dead.

46
00:02:30,436 --> 00:02:35,120
I am going to say what happened to Michelle once, plainly, because you need it to

47
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:39,631
understand everything that follows, and then we are not going to linger on it,

48
00:02:39,631 --> 00:02:42,928
because she was a real young woman and not a plot device.

49
00:02:43,228 --> 00:02:46,850
She had been raped, and she had been stabbed, and she had been strangled.

50
00:02:47,150 --> 00:02:48,357
That is the crime.

51
00:02:48,657 --> 00:02:52,606
Now hold on to three quiet facts about that apartment, because they are going to

52
00:02:52,606 --> 00:02:55,716
matter more than anything the police do for the next two years.

53
00:02:56,016 --> 00:02:57,456
The apartment was small.

54
00:02:57,756 --> 00:03:01,750
There was no sign of forced entry — whoever did this was let in.

55
00:03:02,050 --> 00:03:07,199
And the wounds, a medical examiner would note, were tight, clustered, uniform — the

56
00:03:07,199 --> 00:03:10,177
signature of one hand, one weapon, one attacker.

57
00:03:10,477 --> 00:03:11,452
One attacker.

58
00:03:11,752 --> 00:03:12,959
Remember that number.

59
00:03:13,259 --> 00:03:16,556
It is the only number in this case the evidence ever supported.

60
00:03:16,856 --> 00:03:21,036
The police needed a suspect, and grief moves fast in a small apartment block.

61
00:03:21,336 --> 00:03:25,972
A friend of Michelle's, answering questions in those first awful hours, mentioned a

62
00:03:25,972 --> 00:03:29,324
neighbor from the building — a sailor named Danial Williams.

63
00:03:29,624 --> 00:03:30,971
He was twenty-five.

64
00:03:31,271 --> 00:03:32,804
He had no criminal record.

65
00:03:33,104 --> 00:03:36,912
But he had seemed, she said, a little too interested in Michelle.

66
00:03:37,212 --> 00:03:38,373
That was enough.

67
00:03:38,673 --> 00:03:43,661
Within hours of the body being found, before the physical evidence had said a single

68
00:03:43,661 --> 00:03:48,590
word, Danial Williams was in a small room at the Norfolk police department, and the

69
00:03:48,590 --> 00:03:49,540
door was closed.

70
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:54,020
What happened in that room took roughly eleven hours, and it ran through the night.

71
00:03:54,320 --> 00:03:58,199
Understand what an interrogation like that is actually built to do, because

72
00:03:58,199 --> 00:04:00,682
everything in this story flows downhill from it.

73
00:04:00,982 --> 00:04:03,025
It is not built to discover things.

74
00:04:03,325 --> 00:04:05,183
It is built to conclude them.

75
00:04:05,483 --> 00:04:07,201
The room is small on purpose.

76
00:04:07,501 --> 00:04:09,637
The chair is uncomfortable on purpose.

77
00:04:09,937 --> 00:04:15,060
There is no clock you can see, no window, no sense of whether it's midnight or

78
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:20,117
morning, and the man across the table is not asking you what happened — he is

79
00:04:20,117 --> 00:04:23,730
telling you what happened and waiting for you to agree.

80
00:04:24,030 --> 00:04:26,677
Investigators told Williams they knew he did it.

81
00:04:26,977 --> 00:04:28,649
They told him they had evidence.

82
00:04:28,949 --> 00:04:33,740
They gave him a polygraph in the middle of the night and then told him he had failed

83
00:04:33,740 --> 00:04:38,303
it — which was false, and, you should know, perfectly legal; American police are

84
00:04:38,303 --> 00:04:43,094
allowed to lie to you about the evidence, and in this room they used that permission

85
00:04:43,094 --> 00:04:43,949
like a crowbar.

86
00:04:44,249 --> 00:04:47,268
Hour after hour, the same accusation from new angles.

87
00:04:47,568 --> 00:04:49,983
Deny it, and the denial is proof you're cold.

88
00:04:50,283 --> 00:04:52,698
Cry, and the tears are proof you're guilty.

89
00:04:52,998 --> 00:04:55,831
There is no answer that opens the door except one.

90
00:04:56,131 --> 00:05:00,874
And somewhere in the small hours, a new instrument came into the room: a homicide

91
00:05:00,874 --> 00:05:02,865
detective named Robert Glenn Ford.

92
00:05:03,165 --> 00:05:06,184
Hold on to that name — this film has plans for it.

93
00:05:06,484 --> 00:05:11,500
Ford took over the questioning, and Ford brought the hammer: the death penalty.

94
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:14,076
Confess, and you live to fight it in court.

95
00:05:14,376 --> 00:05:17,441
Keep denying it, and Virginia straps you to a table.

96
00:05:17,741 --> 00:05:22,489
Innocence, at hour nine, stops feeling like protection and starts feeling like a

97
00:05:22,489 --> 00:05:23,082
fatal bet.

98
00:05:23,382 --> 00:05:28,217
A federal judge would one day describe, in a written opinion, how statements in this

99
00:05:28,217 --> 00:05:32,995
case were taken again and again until they said what the police wanted them to say.

100
00:05:33,295 --> 00:05:35,710
But that judgment was nineteen years away.

101
00:05:36,010 --> 00:05:40,900
That night, there was only an exhausted young sailor who had never been arrested for

102
00:05:40,900 --> 00:05:45,732
anything, a locked room, and a man with a badge telling him the only way to see the

103
00:05:45,732 --> 00:05:47,945
sun again ran through the word guilty.

104
00:05:48,245 --> 00:05:52,657
Before dawn on July 9th, Danial Williams confessed to a murder he did not commit.

105
00:05:52,957 --> 00:05:55,093
He had been in that room through the night.

106
00:05:55,393 --> 00:06:00,043
The murder was barely a day old, the physical evidence hadn't even been processed

107
00:06:00,043 --> 00:06:03,659
yet, and the case, as far as Norfolk was concerned, was solved.

108
00:06:03,959 --> 00:06:08,417
And here is the detail that should have ended the case on the spot: he got it wrong.

109
00:06:08,717 --> 00:06:12,014
His account of how Michelle died didn't match how Michelle died.

110
00:06:12,314 --> 00:06:13,800
The method was wrong.

111
00:06:14,100 --> 00:06:15,633
The details were wrong.

112
00:06:15,933 --> 00:06:20,953
So the statement was taken again, and corrected, and taken again, until it finally

113
00:06:20,953 --> 00:06:22,667
agreed with the crime scene.

114
00:06:22,967 --> 00:06:24,546
Read that back slowly.

115
00:06:24,846 --> 00:06:29,179
The confession did not describe the crime; the crime was used to repair the

116
00:06:29,179 --> 00:06:29,815
confession.

117
00:06:30,115 --> 00:06:31,415
Confession number one.

118
00:06:31,715 --> 00:06:32,876
Mark the wall.

119
00:06:33,176 --> 00:06:37,119
One more thing about Danial Williams, because this case keeps finding new floors

120
00:06:37,119 --> 00:06:38,006
beneath the floor.

121
00:06:38,306 --> 00:06:43,289
While he sat in a cell that autumn, charged with capital murder, his young wife was

122
00:06:43,289 --> 00:06:44,250
dying of cancer.

123
00:06:44,550 --> 00:06:45,850
She died that November.

124
00:06:46,150 --> 00:06:47,357
He was not there.

125
00:06:47,657 --> 00:06:50,443
He was awaiting trial for someone else's crime.

126
00:06:52,243 --> 00:06:57,529
If Danial Williams had actually killed Michelle Moore-Bosko, this is where the story

127
00:06:57,529 --> 00:07:00,927
would end — a confession, a conviction, a closed file.

128
00:07:01,227 --> 00:07:04,664
Instead, in December 1997, the DNA came back.

129
00:07:04,964 --> 00:07:06,264
It was not him.

130
00:07:06,564 --> 00:07:11,848
The biology recovered from the scene — the kind of evidence that does not get tired

131
00:07:11,848 --> 00:07:17,195
at 3 a.m., does not fear the death penalty, and cannot be corrected across the table

132
00:07:17,195 --> 00:07:19,614
— excluded Danial Williams completely.

133
00:07:19,914 --> 00:07:23,118
In a working investigation, that is the fire alarm.

134
00:07:23,418 --> 00:07:27,916
It means the confession you built your case on is contaminated, and the killer is

135
00:07:27,916 --> 00:07:28,805
still out there.

136
00:07:29,105 --> 00:07:33,720
There is a version of this story where Norfolk hears that alarm, and Michelle's

137
00:07:33,720 --> 00:07:38,393
actual murderer — a name you will meet soon enough — is identified within weeks.

138
00:07:38,693 --> 00:07:40,226
That is not what happened.

139
00:07:40,526 --> 00:07:44,792
What happened is the single most important sentence in this film: when the DNA

140
00:07:44,792 --> 00:07:48,839
cleared the man who confessed, the police did not question the confession.

141
00:07:49,139 --> 00:07:51,089
They questioned the DNA's loneliness.

142
00:07:51,389 --> 00:07:56,117
Someone else, they decided, must have been there too — someone whose DNA got left

143
00:07:56,117 --> 00:07:57,751
behind instead of Williams'.

144
00:07:58,051 --> 00:08:01,162
And who do you look at when your suspect needs an accomplice?

145
00:08:01,462 --> 00:08:02,484
His roommate.

146
00:08:02,784 --> 00:08:08,064
Joseph Dick was barely twenty, by every account a pliable, gentle, easily-led young

147
00:08:08,064 --> 00:08:12,072
man — the kind of kid who says sorry when you step on his foot.

148
00:08:12,372 --> 00:08:17,375
In January 1998 he went in to face the same detective, and met the same machinery:

149
00:08:17,375 --> 00:08:21,706
we know you did it, you failed the tests, you will die unless you talk.

150
00:08:22,006 --> 00:08:26,789
Navy duty records placed him aboard his ship when Michelle was killed — an alibi

151
00:08:26,789 --> 00:08:31,573
most defendants would kill for, stamped and filed by the United States military.

152
00:08:31,873 --> 00:08:33,173
It did not matter.

153
00:08:33,473 --> 00:08:35,609
Nobody in that room wanted it to matter.

154
00:08:35,909 --> 00:08:39,988
After hours of Ford's method, Joseph Dick confessed to a crime his own service

155
00:08:39,988 --> 00:08:42,132
records said he could not have committed.

156
00:08:42,432 --> 00:08:47,229
And then he did something even sadder, something the psychologists who study false

157
00:08:47,229 --> 00:08:51,441
confessions consider the deepest form of the injury: he kept confessing.

158
00:08:51,741 --> 00:08:54,481
The story he had been handed grew roots in him.

159
00:08:54,781 --> 00:08:59,779
He repeated it to prosecutors, repeated it to himself, and would eventually stand up

160
00:08:59,779 --> 00:09:04,480
in court and testify to it — against other innocent men, in words that had been

161
00:09:04,480 --> 00:09:06,205
written for him in that room.

162
00:09:06,505 --> 00:09:09,802
That is what a single night in that room does to a twenty-year-old.

163
00:09:10,102 --> 00:09:12,238
It doesn't just make him say the thing.

164
00:09:12,538 --> 00:09:13,978
It can make him carry it.

165
00:09:14,278 --> 00:09:15,671
Confession number two.

166
00:09:15,971 --> 00:09:17,039
Mark the wall.

167
00:09:17,339 --> 00:09:20,125
In March 1998, Dick's DNA came back.

168
00:09:20,425 --> 00:09:21,540
Not him either.

169
00:09:21,840 --> 00:09:26,019
Now, you might reasonably ask: why keep the confessions at all, once the science

170
00:09:26,019 --> 00:09:27,273
keeps spitting them out?

171
00:09:27,573 --> 00:09:30,963
Here is the ugly, practical answer every prosecutor knows.

172
00:09:31,263 --> 00:09:33,399
To a jury, a confession is the sun.

173
00:09:33,699 --> 00:09:36,160
It outshines everything placed beside it.

174
00:09:36,460 --> 00:09:41,862
Alibis wilt next to it; even DNA, in 1998, could be talked around — a defendant who

175
00:09:41,862 --> 00:09:46,352
described the crime in his own voice must know something, mustn't he?

176
00:09:46,652 --> 00:09:49,160
A confession is not just evidence of guilt.

177
00:09:49,460 --> 00:09:52,850
It is a finished story, and finished stories win trials.

178
00:09:53,150 --> 00:09:57,916
Which means every man that room broke became a weapon against the next man, and the

179
00:09:57,916 --> 00:10:00,673
file grew heavier exactly as it grew more false.

180
00:10:00,973 --> 00:10:04,270
By now the pattern is locking in where you can watch it work.

181
00:10:04,570 --> 00:10:06,706
Each exclusion should have been an exit.

182
00:10:07,006 --> 00:10:09,653
Each time, the theory simply grew another man.

183
00:10:09,953 --> 00:10:14,272
Two attackers became three, because two didn't explain the DNA either.

184
00:10:14,572 --> 00:10:19,478
Dick, pressed to explain why his biology wasn't in that apartment, produced more

185
00:10:19,478 --> 00:10:24,506
names — names of sailors he barely knew, because the room does not accept "I don't

186
00:10:24,506 --> 00:10:25,671
know" as an answer.

187
00:10:25,971 --> 00:10:31,731
In April 1998 a sailor named Eric Wilson was questioned for about nine hours and

188
00:10:31,731 --> 00:10:35,259
gave them confession number three; mark the wall.

189
00:10:35,559 --> 00:10:37,649
In June, his DNA was excluded.

190
00:10:37,949 --> 00:10:42,448
Eight days later, Derek Tice was arrested down in Florida, and after his own

191
00:10:42,448 --> 00:10:45,704
marathon in that room he became confession number four.

192
00:10:46,004 --> 00:10:47,026
Mark the wall.

193
00:10:47,326 --> 00:10:50,437
Tice, pushed for still more names, gave them more names.

194
00:10:50,737 --> 00:10:55,451
Three additional sailors — Richard Pauley, Geoffrey Farris, John Danser — were

195
00:10:55,451 --> 00:10:59,561
charged with capital murder on nothing but the words of the coerced.

196
00:10:59,861 --> 00:11:05,198
Sit with that: no confession of their own, no witness, no physical trace — arrested

197
00:11:05,198 --> 00:11:09,892
for the death penalty on sentences other men had been broken into saying.

198
00:11:10,192 --> 00:11:14,895
None of the three ever confessed, which by this point almost reads as a scientific

199
00:11:14,895 --> 00:11:18,737
finding: the confessions tracked the interrogations, not the crime.

200
00:11:19,037 --> 00:11:24,034
Danser's paperwork put him three hundred miles away in Pennsylvania on the night —

201
00:11:24,034 --> 00:11:28,604
time cards, bank records, the boring, beautiful proof of an ordinary alibi.

202
00:11:28,904 --> 00:11:31,133
He was charged with capital murder anyway.

203
00:11:31,433 --> 00:11:32,826
Count it with me.

204
00:11:33,126 --> 00:11:38,652
Seven United States Navy sailors, charged with one rape and one murder — a crime the

205
00:11:38,652 --> 00:11:44,178
medical examiner's own findings said was committed by one person, in an apartment so

206
00:11:44,178 --> 00:11:49,441
small a two-man fight would have wrecked it, with not one fingerprint, fiber, or

207
00:11:49,441 --> 00:11:52,073
cell of any of the seven anywhere in it.

208
00:11:52,373 --> 00:11:57,541
The state's theory now required seven men to have taken turns in that tiny room and

209
00:11:57,541 --> 00:12:02,647
to have left, between them, no trace at all — while somehow the trace of an eighth

210
00:12:02,647 --> 00:12:06,259
man, a man police weren't even looking at, was everywhere.

211
00:12:06,559 --> 00:12:11,611
A federal judge would eventually hold this theory up to the light and describe its

212
00:12:11,611 --> 00:12:16,416
absurdity in plain English: multiple attackers, one shared weapon, wounds of a

213
00:12:16,416 --> 00:12:21,221
single uniform depth, and none of the supposed seven leaving so much as a cell

214
00:12:21,221 --> 00:12:21,652
behind.

215
00:12:21,952 --> 00:12:23,995
Physics testified in this case.

216
00:12:24,295 --> 00:12:26,106
Nobody called it to the stand.

217
00:12:26,406 --> 00:12:31,848
In February 1999, the laboratory finished the arithmetic: all seven charged men,

218
00:12:31,848 --> 00:12:34,161
every single one, excluded by DNA.

219
00:12:34,461 --> 00:12:35,668
Seven charges.

220
00:12:35,968 --> 00:12:37,036
Zero matches.

221
00:12:37,336 --> 00:12:39,147
And still nobody walked free.

222
00:12:39,447 --> 00:12:43,584
The doors stayed shut, the trials stayed on the calendar, and the death penalty

223
00:12:43,584 --> 00:12:47,511
stayed on the table — because by then, admitting the truth would have meant

224
00:12:47,511 --> 00:12:49,710
admitting what had been done in that room.

225
00:12:50,010 --> 00:12:51,264
Keep your tally.

226
00:12:51,564 --> 00:12:54,815
Four confessions on the wall, all of them false.

227
00:12:55,115 --> 00:12:59,666
The fifth confession is coming — and it does not come from an interrogation room.

228
00:12:59,966 --> 00:13:01,313
It comes in the mail.

229
00:13:03,113 --> 00:13:08,090
In February 1999, a letter left a Virginia prison cell in an envelope, written by an

230
00:13:08,090 --> 00:13:11,704
inmate named Omar Ballard to a woman he knew back in Norfolk.

231
00:13:12,004 --> 00:13:15,069
Ballard was exactly the man police are supposed to find.

232
00:13:15,369 --> 00:13:19,705
He had known Michelle — a federal court would later note plainly that they were

233
00:13:19,705 --> 00:13:22,614
friends, that he had often been inside her apartment.

234
00:13:22,914 --> 00:13:26,025
He was the someone-she-knew her door opened to.

235
00:13:26,325 --> 00:13:28,136
And his violence was not hidden.

236
00:13:28,436 --> 00:13:33,177
Days before Michelle was killed, a woman at the same apartment complex had been

237
00:13:33,177 --> 00:13:34,798
savagely beaten with a bat.

238
00:13:35,098 --> 00:13:38,163
Days after, a fourteen-year-old girl was raped a mile away.

239
00:13:38,463 --> 00:13:39,810
Both were Ballard.

240
00:13:40,110 --> 00:13:44,290
The second crime is why he was in prison, months later, holding a pen.

241
00:13:44,590 --> 00:13:49,641
What he wrote, in his own spelling, in his own hand, is quoted in the federal

242
00:13:49,641 --> 00:13:55,151
record, and I'll read it to you exactly: "And one last thing you Remember that night

243
00:13:55,151 --> 00:14:00,333
i went to Mommie's house and the Next morning Michelle got killed guess who did

244
00:14:00,333 --> 00:14:01,448
that, Me HA, HA."

245
00:14:01,748 --> 00:14:03,095
Guess who did that.

246
00:14:03,395 --> 00:14:03,999
Me.

247
00:14:04,299 --> 00:14:05,506
Ha, ha.

248
00:14:05,806 --> 00:14:07,385
The letter reached the police.

249
00:14:07,685 --> 00:14:12,092
And now watch the machine do, instantly, everything it had refused to do for two

250
00:14:12,092 --> 00:14:12,422
years.

251
00:14:12,722 --> 00:14:15,648
Ballard's DNA was tested in March 1999.

252
00:14:15,948 --> 00:14:17,063
It matched.

253
00:14:17,363 --> 00:14:22,646
Not partially, not arguably — it matched the swabs, the fingernail scrapings, the

254
00:14:22,646 --> 00:14:23,168
blanket.

255
00:14:23,468 --> 00:14:27,608
He remains, to this day, the only human being whose DNA was ever found at that

256
00:14:27,608 --> 00:14:27,926
scene.

257
00:14:28,226 --> 00:14:33,385
Brought in, he confessed freely — no eleven hours required — and his confession

258
00:14:33,385 --> 00:14:38,675
needed no repairs — the details fit the apartment, fit the wounds, fit the night.

259
00:14:38,975 --> 00:14:42,597
And he said one more thing, twice, once in each statement he gave.

260
00:14:42,897 --> 00:14:44,058
He did it alone.

261
00:14:44,358 --> 00:14:45,473
No sailors.

262
00:14:45,773 --> 00:14:47,027
No accomplices.

263
00:14:47,327 --> 00:14:51,989
Alone — exactly what the locked door, the small rooms, and the uniform wounds had

264
00:14:51,989 --> 00:14:54,061
been saying since the first morning.

265
00:14:54,361 --> 00:14:55,708
The fifth confession.

266
00:14:56,008 --> 00:14:57,355
The only true one.

267
00:14:57,655 --> 00:15:03,137
The wall now reads: four lies extracted by professionals across hours of coercion —

268
00:15:03,137 --> 00:15:06,571
and one truth, mailed in, voluntarily, with a laugh.

269
00:15:06,871 --> 00:15:09,982
So they opened the cell doors and let the sailors go home.

270
00:15:10,282 --> 00:15:11,489
You know they didn't.

271
00:15:11,789 --> 00:15:15,888
This is the part of the story that separates it from every ordinary wrongful

272
00:15:15,888 --> 00:15:19,933
conviction, the part a federal judge would later stare at in something like

273
00:15:19,933 --> 00:15:20,473
disbelief.

274
00:15:20,773 --> 00:15:26,151
Presented with the real killer, matched by DNA, confessing voluntarily and alone,

275
00:15:26,151 --> 00:15:28,807
the prosecution did not drop its theory.

276
00:15:29,107 --> 00:15:30,686
It enlarged it — again.

277
00:15:30,986 --> 00:15:34,190
Omar Ballard, they now announced, was simply the eighth man.

278
00:15:34,490 --> 00:15:40,144
The sailors' confessions — the ones that had been wrong until corrected — were still

279
00:15:40,144 --> 00:15:40,481
good.

280
00:15:40,781 --> 00:15:46,084
The man every shred of physical evidence pointed to was folded into the story as one

281
00:15:46,084 --> 00:15:51,323
more accomplice, over his own repeated insistence that he had acted entirely alone.

282
00:15:51,623 --> 00:15:52,273
Why?

283
00:15:52,573 --> 00:15:57,449
Not because anyone produced new evidence — there was none, there was never any.

284
00:15:57,749 --> 00:16:03,038
The honest answer is smaller and colder than a conspiracy: by 1999, the case had a

285
00:16:03,038 --> 00:16:05,876
momentum that truth couldn't afford to stop.

286
00:16:06,176 --> 00:16:08,219
One man had already pleaded guilty.

287
00:16:08,519 --> 00:16:12,528
Careers, verdicts, and a grieving family's certainty were all built on the

288
00:16:12,528 --> 00:16:13,395
confession wall.

289
00:16:13,695 --> 00:16:18,560
Tearing it down meant a prosecutor standing in front of Norfolk and saying: we broke

290
00:16:18,560 --> 00:16:23,193
four innocent sailors — and the real killer was still free days after we had our

291
00:16:23,193 --> 00:16:28,058
first confession signed, free when a fourteen-year-old girl was attacked a mile from

292
00:16:28,058 --> 00:16:28,927
that apartment.

293
00:16:29,227 --> 00:16:31,735
No one said that sentence in 1999.

294
00:16:32,035 --> 00:16:37,085
It would take seventeen more years and a federal courtroom before anyone with power

295
00:16:37,085 --> 00:16:38,119
said it out loud.

296
00:16:38,419 --> 00:16:42,831
And the cruelest bookkeeping of all is in the dates, so look at them with me.

297
00:16:43,131 --> 00:16:47,844
Danial Williams had already pleaded guilty that January — a month before the letter

298
00:16:47,844 --> 00:16:52,557
— because his lawyers told him, correctly, that it was the plea or a probable death

299
00:16:52,557 --> 00:16:53,069
sentence.

300
00:16:53,369 --> 00:16:56,527
But Joseph Dick pleaded guilty in April 1999.

301
00:16:56,827 --> 00:16:57,616
April.

302
00:16:57,916 --> 00:16:58,938
After the letter.

303
00:16:59,238 --> 00:17:00,445
After the match.

304
00:17:00,745 --> 00:17:05,207
The Commonwealth accepted a guilty plea for life in prison from a DNA-excluded man a

305
00:17:05,207 --> 00:17:08,872
month and a half after its own laboratory identified the real killer.

306
00:17:09,172 --> 00:17:13,518
In May, the charges against Pauley, Farris, and Danser were quietly dropped — the

307
00:17:13,518 --> 00:17:17,113
theory shrinking back down now that it had its convictions in hand.

308
00:17:17,413 --> 00:17:22,775
That June, a jury acquitted Eric Wilson of murder but convicted him of rape on the

309
00:17:22,775 --> 00:17:27,026
strength of his coerced statement; he got eight and a half years.

310
00:17:27,326 --> 00:17:32,651
Derek Tice, who refused to fold, was convicted in February 2000 and sentenced to two

311
00:17:32,651 --> 00:17:37,976
life terms; the verdict was thrown out on appeal, and Norfolk simply tried him again

312
00:17:37,976 --> 00:17:40,004
in 2003 and convicted him again.

313
00:17:40,304 --> 00:17:45,143
And in March 2000, in the same courthouse system, Omar Ballard pleaded guilty to

314
00:17:45,143 --> 00:17:50,043
Michelle's rape and murder and took two life sentences, telling the court what he

315
00:17:50,043 --> 00:17:52,100
had told everyone: he acted alone.

316
00:17:52,400 --> 00:17:54,536
Hold both of those in your head at once.

317
00:17:54,836 --> 00:17:59,477
The State of Virginia accepted a guilty plea from Omar Ballard, who swore under oath

318
00:17:59,477 --> 00:18:03,952
that he had done this alone — while keeping four other men in prison for doing it

319
00:18:03,952 --> 00:18:04,449
with him.

320
00:18:04,749 --> 00:18:08,093
One more thing belongs here, and it needs to be said gently.

321
00:18:08,393 --> 00:18:10,622
Michelle's parents believed the confessions.

322
00:18:10,922 --> 00:18:15,444
Of course they did — four men had said the words, in detail, on paper, and the

323
00:18:15,444 --> 00:18:19,328
people who extracted those words assured her family they were true.

324
00:18:19,628 --> 00:18:24,168
Her mother and father spent those years, and many after, certain the sailors were

325
00:18:24,168 --> 00:18:27,755
their daughter's killers, and fighting to keep them behind bars.

326
00:18:28,055 --> 00:18:29,959
That is not a failing in them.

327
00:18:30,259 --> 00:18:35,300
It is the second cruelty of a false confession: it doesn't just cage the innocent,

328
00:18:35,300 --> 00:18:38,804
it hands a grieving family a lie to build their grief on.

329
00:18:39,104 --> 00:18:42,866
Whatever anger you're feeling right now — aim it at the method.

330
00:18:43,166 --> 00:18:44,281
Never at them.

331
00:18:46,081 --> 00:18:47,010
Years, then.

332
00:18:47,310 --> 00:18:51,675
That's what innocence costs once a system has decided to protect its own story.

333
00:18:51,975 --> 00:18:54,529
Williams, inside since 1997.

334
00:18:54,829 --> 00:18:56,779
Dick, since 1998.

335
00:18:57,079 --> 00:18:58,704
Tice, convicted twice.

336
00:18:59,004 --> 00:19:03,975
Wilson finished his sentence and walked out in 2005 into a different cage — a rape

337
00:19:03,975 --> 00:19:09,067
conviction meant the sex-offender registry, and the registry meant, among a thousand

338
00:19:09,067 --> 00:19:12,704
daily humiliations, that he could not adopt his own stepson.

339
00:19:13,004 --> 00:19:18,197
Add it up across the four of them and you get more than forty years of stolen time,

340
00:19:18,197 --> 00:19:22,013
for a crime the state's own files proved was one other man's.

341
00:19:22,313 --> 00:19:27,291
But out in the world, the case had begun to rot in public view — because the file,

342
00:19:27,291 --> 00:19:29,233
honestly read, is not ambiguous.

343
00:19:29,533 --> 00:19:30,648
It never was.

344
00:19:30,948 --> 00:19:35,751
Any competent stranger who sat down with it saw the same three columns: confessions

345
00:19:35,751 --> 00:19:40,612
that contradicted the scene until they were fixed, DNA that cleared every sailor and

346
00:19:40,612 --> 00:19:45,183
convicted exactly one man, and that man on record, under oath, saying he did it

347
00:19:45,183 --> 00:19:45,530
alone.

348
00:19:45,830 --> 00:19:50,056
In 2005, lawyers filed clemency petitions on the governor's desk.

349
00:19:50,356 --> 00:19:55,436
In 2008 came a book about the case called The Wrong Guys, co-written by one of the

350
00:19:55,436 --> 00:19:59,897
country's leading false-confession scholars, and then something close to

351
00:19:59,897 --> 00:20:04,791
unprecedented: a public letter from twenty-six retired FBI agents — men who had

352
00:20:04,791 --> 00:20:09,933
spent entire careers inside interrogation rooms, men whose professional religion is

353
00:20:09,933 --> 00:20:15,075
the confession — telling Virginia's governor the convictions were a tragic mistake,

354
00:20:15,075 --> 00:20:19,102
that the confessions had never been checked against the evidence.

355
00:20:19,402 --> 00:20:22,281
Ten former Virginia attorneys general joined.

356
00:20:22,581 --> 00:20:24,067
Retired judges.

357
00:20:24,367 --> 00:20:25,760
Former prosecutors.

358
00:20:26,060 --> 00:20:31,212
Even jurors from the original trials — thirteen of them — saying publicly: we were

359
00:20:31,212 --> 00:20:32,469
not shown the truth.

360
00:20:32,769 --> 00:20:37,174
When the people who built the machine start writing letters against the machine, you

361
00:20:37,174 --> 00:20:40,478
are watching something rare, and you should slow down and look.

362
00:20:40,778 --> 00:20:45,564
In August 2009, Governor Tim Kaine granted conditional pardons, and three men walked

363
00:20:45,564 --> 00:20:48,812
out of prison after roughly eleven and twelve years each.

364
00:20:49,112 --> 00:20:53,617
Watch the word conditional, though, because this is a half-open door, not an ending.

365
00:20:53,917 --> 00:20:55,357
Their convictions stood.

366
00:20:55,657 --> 00:20:56,957
They were felons.

367
00:20:57,257 --> 00:21:01,674
They registered, all of them, as sex offenders — reporting their addresses,

368
00:21:01,674 --> 00:21:06,503
photographed for the registries, flagged in every background check, treated by the

369
00:21:06,503 --> 00:21:11,096
paperwork of daily life as the predators someone else had scripted them to be.

370
00:21:11,396 --> 00:21:13,718
Try renting an apartment like that.

371
00:21:14,018 --> 00:21:18,793
Try explaining it at a job interview: yes, capital murder, but conditionally

372
00:21:18,793 --> 00:21:19,359
pardoned.

373
00:21:19,659 --> 00:21:24,382
Kaine himself, releasing them, pointedly declined to call them innocent — the

374
00:21:24,382 --> 00:21:27,879
state's mercy arrived with its accusation still attached.

375
00:21:28,179 --> 00:21:32,955
In November 2010, PBS aired a Frontline documentary about the case that much of

376
00:21:32,955 --> 00:21:37,973
America found unwatchable in the best sense — and understand this: when that famous

377
00:21:37,973 --> 00:21:41,600
film ended, the story you are hearing now had no ending yet.

378
00:21:41,900 --> 00:21:45,940
The film could not tell you the four were innocent, because no court had said so.

379
00:21:46,240 --> 00:21:50,141
It could not tell you what happened to the detective, because that verdict was

380
00:21:50,141 --> 00:21:52,742
thirteen days old and the sentence hadn't come down.

381
00:21:53,042 --> 00:21:55,689
Everything that follows happened after the credits rolled.

382
00:21:55,989 --> 00:21:59,008
Here is what followed, in the order the truth finally won.

383
00:21:59,308 --> 00:22:04,505
In 2011, the federal courts finished with Derek Tice: his conviction was gone — his

384
00:22:04,505 --> 00:22:09,764
lawyer's failure to challenge that coerced confession was fatal to the verdict — and

385
00:22:09,764 --> 00:22:13,333
that August, prosecutors gave up and dropped the charges.

386
00:22:13,633 --> 00:22:14,748
One name clean.

387
00:22:15,048 --> 00:22:20,188
In September 2016, the remaining two men's case landed in front of a federal judge

388
00:22:20,188 --> 00:22:25,454
named John A. Gibney Jr. He did what nobody in Norfolk had done in 1997: he actually

389
00:22:25,454 --> 00:22:30,594
held the confessions up against the evidence, in a two-day hearing, on the record,

390
00:22:30,594 --> 00:22:34,042
with the whole nineteen-year file open in front of him.

391
00:22:34,342 --> 00:22:39,498
And then he did something federal judges almost never do — because the law only asks

392
00:22:39,498 --> 00:22:41,401
them to find errors, not truth.

393
00:22:41,701 --> 00:22:45,788
He ruled that Danial Williams and Joseph Dick were not merely entitled to new

394
00:22:45,788 --> 00:22:46,159
trials.

395
00:22:46,459 --> 00:22:48,038
They were actually innocent.

396
00:22:48,338 --> 00:22:52,685
His written words belong on screen: "By any measure, the evidence shows the

397
00:22:52,685 --> 00:22:57,495
defendants' innocence — by a preponderance of the evidence, by clear and convincing

398
00:22:57,495 --> 00:23:02,131
evidence, by evidence beyond a reasonable doubt, or even by conclusive evidence.

399
00:23:02,431 --> 00:23:07,589
Elsewhere in the opinion he put it more bluntly — no sane human being, he wrote,

400
00:23:07,589 --> 00:23:09,072
could find them guilty.

401
00:23:09,372 --> 00:23:13,841
That October, Virginia's own attorney general stopped defending the convictions, and

402
00:23:13,841 --> 00:23:14,852
Gibney erased them.

403
00:23:15,152 --> 00:23:19,950
In March 2017, Governor Terry McAuliffe granted absolute pardons to all four men —

404
00:23:19,950 --> 00:23:24,748
including Eric Wilson, who had no court left to turn to, because he'd finished his

405
00:23:24,748 --> 00:23:26,855
sentence before the truth caught up.

406
00:23:27,155 --> 00:23:31,925
Twenty years after that first eleven-hour night, the last conviction dissolved, the

407
00:23:31,925 --> 00:23:36,465
registry entries were wiped, and the words the record now carries next to their

408
00:23:36,465 --> 00:23:39,740
names are the ones a judge had already written: innocent.

409
00:23:40,040 --> 00:23:42,594
In 2018, the money followed the words.

410
00:23:42,894 --> 00:23:46,238
The Commonwealth of Virginia paid three and a half million dollars.

411
00:23:46,538 --> 00:23:49,464
The City of Norfolk paid four point nine million more.

412
00:23:49,764 --> 00:23:54,706
Roughly eight point four million dollars, total — which sounds like a great deal of

413
00:23:54,706 --> 00:23:59,529
money until you divide it by four men and forty-plus stolen years, and realize it

414
00:23:59,529 --> 00:24:03,696
prices an innocent man's year at roughly the cost of a middling house.

415
00:24:03,996 --> 00:24:08,932
Understand what the money is, and what it isn't: no one was ever criminally punished

416
00:24:08,932 --> 00:24:12,634
for what was done to these four men in that interrogation room.

417
00:24:12,934 --> 00:24:17,357
The check is the closest thing to an apology the record contains — and even the

418
00:24:17,357 --> 00:24:18,925
check took twenty-one years.

419
00:24:19,225 --> 00:24:21,083
And now the last tally mark.

420
00:24:21,383 --> 00:24:23,148
I've kept one thing from you.

421
00:24:23,448 --> 00:24:27,966
Robert Glenn Ford — the detective in every one of those rooms, the man the

422
00:24:27,966 --> 00:24:31,018
confessions have in common — had done this before.

423
00:24:31,318 --> 00:24:36,324
In 1990, years before Michelle was killed, Ford extracted confessions from three

424
00:24:36,324 --> 00:24:38,702
teenagers in a Norfolk robbery-murder.

425
00:24:39,002 --> 00:24:40,117
False ones.

426
00:24:40,417 --> 00:24:41,485
It was known.

427
00:24:41,785 --> 00:24:45,454
He was moved off homicide — and then, quietly, moved back.

428
00:24:45,754 --> 00:24:49,191
That is who was waiting in the room when Danial Williams sat down.

429
00:24:49,491 --> 00:24:53,206
Ford was never criminally charged for what he did to the Norfolk Four.

430
00:24:53,506 --> 00:24:58,147
Understand that plainly, because this film doesn't sell false comfort: the system

431
00:24:58,147 --> 00:25:00,611
never punished him for its own catastrophe.

432
00:25:00,911 --> 00:25:05,602
But in 2010 — while the four were still legally rapists and murderers, while

433
00:25:05,602 --> 00:25:10,540
Frontline was still editing its film — federal agents came for Ford on something

434
00:25:10,540 --> 00:25:10,849
else.

435
00:25:11,149 --> 00:25:15,459
For years, it turned out, the veteran detective had been taking cash from criminal

436
00:25:15,459 --> 00:25:17,929
defendants in exchange for favorable treatment.

437
00:25:18,229 --> 00:25:20,644
Extortion, under color of his badge.

438
00:25:20,944 --> 00:25:25,771
And when the FBI asked him about it, he lied to them — a federal crime with its own

439
00:25:25,771 --> 00:25:30,424
delicious symmetry, because Robert Glenn Ford, of all men, was undone by a false

440
00:25:30,424 --> 00:25:33,390
statement made under pressure in an interview room.

441
00:25:33,690 --> 00:25:37,583
A jury convicted him that October, less than two weeks before the Frontline

442
00:25:37,583 --> 00:25:38,102
broadcast.

443
00:25:38,402 --> 00:25:42,711
In February 2011, a federal judge sentenced him to twelve and a half years — a

444
00:25:42,711 --> 00:25:46,854
longer sentence than Eric Wilson served for a rape that never involved him.

445
00:25:47,154 --> 00:25:52,322
The man who filled a wall with false confessions went into a federal prison cell,

446
00:25:52,322 --> 00:25:57,235
and the United States Navy sailors he put away went home, pardoned, paid, and

447
00:25:57,235 --> 00:26:02,531
declared by a federal court to be exactly what they had said they were at hour one,

448
00:26:02,531 --> 00:26:07,827
hour five, and hour ten of that first long night — before hour eleven finally broke

449
00:26:07,827 --> 00:26:08,145
them.

450
00:26:09,945 --> 00:26:12,221
So count the confessions one last time.

451
00:26:12,521 --> 00:26:15,725
Danial Williams — false, extracted before dawn.

452
00:26:16,025 --> 00:26:19,090
Joseph Dick — false, rehearsed until he believed it.

453
00:26:19,390 --> 00:26:21,712
Eric Wilson — false, nine hours.

454
00:26:22,012 --> 00:26:26,424
Derek Tice — false, and he paid for recanting it with two trials.

455
00:26:26,724 --> 00:26:32,294
And Omar Ballard — true, mailed from a prison cell with a taunt, matched by every

456
00:26:32,294 --> 00:26:35,733
piece of DNA in the file, sworn to the end: alone.

457
00:26:36,033 --> 00:26:39,609
Four false confessions took a marathon night apiece to build.

458
00:26:39,909 --> 00:26:44,529
The one true confession arrived by mail, free of charge, and the system that had

459
00:26:44,529 --> 00:26:48,918
sweated through all those nights couldn't bring itself to open the envelope.

460
00:26:49,218 --> 00:26:54,548
That is the lesson of Norfolk, and it is not comfortable: an interrogation room is a

461
00:26:54,548 --> 00:26:57,531
factory, and what it manufactures is agreement.

462
00:26:57,831 --> 00:27:02,630
Get the wrong man inside it for long enough and he will hand you a murder in his own

463
00:27:02,630 --> 00:27:05,772
voice — wrong in every detail until you fix it for him.

464
00:27:06,072 --> 00:27:08,208
The DNA never lied in this case.

465
00:27:08,508 --> 00:27:10,041
Not once, not to anyone.

466
00:27:10,341 --> 00:27:14,521
People simply preferred the confessions, because the confessions were theirs.

467
00:27:14,821 --> 00:27:17,747
Michelle Moore-Bosko would be in her forties now.

468
00:27:18,047 --> 00:27:22,087
Her killer confessed in 1999 and is serving two life sentences.

469
00:27:22,387 --> 00:27:27,161
Her parents grieved twice — once for her, and once for the years they were told a

470
00:27:27,161 --> 00:27:28,517
lie about who took her.

471
00:27:28,817 --> 00:27:33,089
Four sailors gave the Navy their youth; Virginia took the years that followed.

472
00:27:33,389 --> 00:27:38,118
And the detective who called that justice got to test, from the inside, how it feels

473
00:27:38,118 --> 00:27:41,609
when the government decides what your words are going to mean.

474
00:27:41,909 --> 00:27:46,799
If you believe the count matters — that four false confessions should weigh less

